  not published the list of approved  titles for august breaking its routine  of announcing newly licensed games  either in the middle or at the end of  each month since may 2019 between 80 to  100 games are usually approved each  month later on october 1st the  associated press reported that further  restrictions we put onto games this time  regarding in-game content specifically  there would be a ban on quote effeminate  males gay relationships and games that  had no distinct or clear moral choices  now this almost sounds a little jarring  to you if you're anything like me after  work you hop into discord and play a  little bit of everything for as long as  you can before you go to bed  if you go to bed china bans under 18s  from playing video games more than three  hours a week a week  huh  a week but in order to explain how this  happened why it's actually not that  shocking i gotta bring you back and  explain how we got to this point now  it's also important to say that china is  a country of over 1 billion people with  a incredibly long history and a very  complex government the situation is much  more nuanced than a video like this in  cover in the time we have today china  has been worried about adverse reactions  to video games specifically addiction to  them for an example as to why this might  be in 2011 a man lost consciousness in a  gaming cafe outside of beijing after  supported he did not eat or sleep much  for three days he later died according  to the south china morning post this  past march president xi jinping quote  explicitly listed game addiction as a  concern for the psychological health of  the country's teens china has tried to  remedy this with private army-like camps  made to treat web addiction with methods  that include  beatings solitary confinement and  electroshock therapy this has led to  injuries and deaths in the children that  attend these camps with some of these  camp facilitators being brought before a  court while gaming addiction is a  growing problem across the world people  have noticed that this is a symptom of a  much larger issue of toxic work cultures  you know are video games kind of  addictive  yeah absolutely and we're going to talk  about that in a second but i think that  what people here are missing is that  there's a confluence of events that gets  young people  uninterested in participating in society  and then when i choose not to  participate in society because i don't  get paid enough i don't get enough  respect i don't enjoy my job it's not  fulfilling then i choose not to  participate in society and when i choose  not to participate in society what do i  do with all my time and so what i end up  doing with all my time is like video  games these new regulations are also  part of a recent crackdown the chinese  government has had on their tech and  entertainment industries rules and  regulations like this aren't new to the  chinese gaming scene in 2019 similar but  less harsh regulations were put in that  barred miners from playing games from  the hours of 10 pm to 8 am with a time  limit of 90 minutes national holidays  were the exception with a time limit of  three hours fortune also reported that  quote the rules instituted in 2019  created content guidelines that banned  gory images like blood promoted chinese  culture and said gambling-like features  such as loot boxes must be labeled with  the odds of winning censorship is  nothing new to china china has outright  banned media for being shown in the  country that they simply don't want to  be seen some of you may remember when  battlefield 4 was banned for being sold  in the country or when the censorship of  any images that connected president xi  jinping and winnie the pooh winnie the  pooh looking [ __ ] despite all of  this there are still major concerns by  the chinese esports scene in order to  become a pro player you obviously have  to practice your game but if you're only  able to play up to three hours a week  how could you possibly get good or even  learn to like a game now if you're  anything like me you might think that  young kids could possibly dodge this by  using a vpn or a separate adult account  but like i said that could become  increasingly hard when you're forced to  use your real name and information and  the developers are being asked to take  an extra security step by the government  tencent actually announced that they'll  be using ai and facial recognition to  help comply with these new regulations  it must get real hard to swerve in china  and just to show how these regulations  are being upheld fortune reported on an  incident where at 3am a player managed  to get a penta kill in the ever popular  mobile game honor kings a fellow player  noticed that the pentakiller's account  belonged to a 60 year old gaming  profiles in china display birth years  and posted it on china's twitter like  weibo the post went viral and weibo  users concluded that the skilled gamer  was likely not a six-year-old at all but  rather a kid who has swiped a relative's  phone tencent said the gamer's profile  had passed 17 facial recognition age  checks this year still it couldn't  definitely conclude that a sexogenerian  had executed the pentakill and therefore  couldn't rule out that the player had  violated draconian veto game laws that  china had made even stricter on  september 1st with all this being said  chinese players have been working  playing and practicing with these types  of restrictions since 2019 and china's  performance hasn't been bad at all  they've often dominated league of  legends in the past few years and rng  won msi back in may and it's not just  china who's had to practice under these  types of restrictions in 2011 a law was  introduced in south korea that  prohibited anyone under 16 from playing  video games between 12am and 6am that  law either known as the shutdown law or  the cinderella law will actually be  rolled back and allow for parents and  guardians to choose their specific  curfews young players have still come  from internet cafes then to academy  teams and then to the main rosters and  they are all still powerhouses but we  have not seen regulations as strict as  china's most recent development some  people are happy with the regulations  saying this will fix a large problem  regarding kids and games on the other  hand there are people sharing major  concerns with privacy and government  control after all it is your face and  name that will be used to prove your  identity the new york times reported  that an esports professional hau shu who  owns a training center said there are  enough players in the mix to sustain the  scene for now so effects from these  restrictions might not be felt for a  while however the south china morning  post reported that an esports technical  director named warren lee said quote  under this new regulation there is  pretty much zero chance to train the  next generation of esports competitors  personally i can't imagine what this  means for esports team owners in china  right now and he may have a point  in the days after the first regulations  were announced ren lpl reported from a  translated weibo post that the league of  legends development league or ldl was  complying with them by blocking anyone  who was under 18 from competing they  also delayed matches that were supposed  to happen that week as rosters probably  scrambled to fill the now empty spots as  of september 2nd about 31 players have  been dropped from the lpl and ldl for  being under 18. this includes 17 year  old lynn cream john who just won rookie  of the split the lpl 2021 summer split  he has since been removed from his  team's roster on his organization's  website now despite all of this we're  only still about a month into these new  restrictions while there have been  immediate consequences we still don't  know what the long-term effects on the  chinese esports scene will be and the  same goes for the content bands we don't  know how games will be changed to be  released in china if they'll be changed  at all and how this will affect original  chinese games only time will tell how  this will all play out and if china will  place further restrictions on their  games and the people that play them  then on september 9th  there's this other girl who can sing  like  oh god colton have fun with that one  rules and regulations like this aren't  new to the chinese gaming sim scene yeah  the gaming sun  you get a high five danny  not keith though what's something that  winnie the pooh says that's like hey i  said i got any other catchphrase  i suppose you're great  wait why are you so good at it danny  why are you so good at that  yeah right okay honestly

